@h1 Headings & structure h1@

Headings give a document its outline; sectioning wrappers give it a skeleton.
Wit has six heading levels and a set of semantic containers that map straight to
the HTML elements a screen reader, a print engine, or a search index looks for.
You need only the headings to get going — the rest is optional structure you add
when it earns its keep.

@h2 Six heading levels h2@

The heading nodes run from level one to level six. Level one is the document
title; the rest nest under it to show your outline:

@@@pre
@h1 The Lamp Keeper h1@
@h2 Chapter One h2@
@h3 A Quiet Morning h3@
pre@@@

Headings are inline-context, so their text is placed directly inside the tag —
@code <h1>The Lamp Keeper</h1> code@ — with no stray paragraph wrapped around it.
Use one level-one heading as the title and let the deeper levels follow the
structure of your argument; do not skip levels just to get a smaller size (size
is a styling concern, covered in @a |href /docs/write/styling/| Styling a@).

@h2 Sectioning wrappers h2@

For anything longer than a note, semantic wrappers describe the _shape_ of the
page. Each maps to the same-named HTML element:

@@@pre
@article ... article@     a self-contained piece
@section ... section@     a thematic division
@header ... header@       a section's opening (title block)
@footer ... footer@       a section's closing (byline, date)
@nav ... nav@             navigation links
@aside ... aside@         supporting or marginal content
@main ... main@           the primary content region
pre@@@

Put them together and a document gains a real structure:

@@@pre
@article
@header
@h1 A Report on the Northern Lights h1@
header@

@section
The season opened early this year.
section@

@footer
@small Published 2024. small@
footer@
article@
pre@@@

which renders as an @code <article> code@ with a @code <header> code@,
@code <section> code@, and @code <footer> code@ nested inside — exactly the
outline you wrote. These wrappers are optional: reach for them when the structure
helps a reader or an assistive technology, and skip them when plain headings are
enough.

@h2 Generic containers h2@

When you want a box to attribute or style but none of the semantic wrappers fits,
reach for the generic block and inline containers:

@@@pre
@div |class card|
A boxed aside, grouped for styling.
div@

An @span |id lede| opening span span@ inside a sentence.
pre@@@

The block container becomes a @code <div> code@; the inline one becomes a
@code <span> code@. Both accept only two attributes — an identifier and a class —
which is enough to hook them for styling. See
@a |href /docs/write/styling/| Styling a@ for how a class name reaches your CSS.

@h2 Indentation is for your eye h2@

Nesting comes from matching open and close names, never from columns. You can
indent your source however reads best; the structure is identical either way,
and @code wit fmt code@ will re-indent a file to match its real nesting whenever
you like.

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li _Wrapping section prose in a paragraph node._ A @@@code @section code@@@
already paragraph-wraps its text, so an explicit @@@code @p … p@ code@@@ inside
is redundant. Write bare prose inside a section. li@
@li _A comment as the first line of a container body._ A leading comment inside a
@@@code @header code@@@ or @@@code @section code@@@ can collapse the heading right
after it. Leave a blank line after the comment — see
@a |href /docs/write/comments/| Comments a@. li@
@li _Skipping heading levels for size._ Jumping from level one to level four to
get a smaller heading breaks the outline. Keep the levels meaningful and adjust
size in styling instead. li@
ul@

